ZERO browser / accessibility-first network client

PURPOSE
  First concrete software target of ZERO.
  Build an accessible browser able to reach Tor services while exposing a screen-reader-native interface.

CONSTRAINTS
  No Python.
  No Rust.
  No JavaScript.
  No OCR as the primary accessibility mechanism.
  No dependence on an existing screen reader as the core accessibility layer.
  Tor connectivity must use the standard Tor protocol/client boundary; never expose or implement onion-service abuse controls as a bypass.

ARCHITECTURE
  NETWORK
    HTTP(S) and Tor-aware transport are isolated from presentation.
  DOCUMENT
    Parse pages into a semantic document tree.
  ACCESS
    Convert the document tree into stable roles, names, states and relationships.
  SPEECH
    Native speech queue with interruption, priority and replay.
  BRAILLE
    Parallel semantic stream; never derived from speech output.
  KEYBOARD
    Complete keyboard navigation with deterministic focus movement.
  HISTORY
    Navigation history is structural state, not opaque screen pixels.
  UNKNOWN
    Unsupported or ambiguous semantics remain explicit.

SCREEN-READER CORE
  The browser must contain its own accessibility engine.
  It exposes:
    heading navigation
    landmark navigation
    link navigation
    form controls
    tables
    lists
    dialogs
    status changes
    focus changes
    document position
    selection
    errors
  Speech and braille consume the same semantic event stream.

SECURITY
  Do not weaken Tor anonymity guarantees.
  Never silently reveal onion destinations, credentials, cookies or private keys.
  Keep network identity, document identity and accessibility state separated.
  All privileged operations require explicit user action.

SUCCESS
  A blind user can install, start, connect to Tor, navigate a document,
  move through its semantic structure, enter a form, hear changes,
  and recover focus entirely from keyboard and screen-reader output.
